From 227f8b678b0f7783cdaff1a57f8338398b2100ff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: osy <osy@turing.llc>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 02:15:31 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] arm/hvf: serve PMCCNTR_EL0 reads without the BQL

Windows on Arm reads the PMU cycle counter constantly -- its x64
emulator implements RDTSC on it -- and HVF cannot virtualize the PMU, so
under an emulated game the guest takes several hundred thousand
PMCCNTR_EL0 traps per second (measured 640k/s at load, ~300k/s in play).
Each went through the general exit path: BQL, vtimer sync, and a return
to the vCPU thread loop.  Serve the common case -- counter enabled,
unfiltered, 64-bit -- from the exit handler with no lock and no state
sync, and go straight back into the guest unless something is pending; a
kick that races is sticky in HVF and surfaces as a CANCELED exit on the
next run.

The counter state the fast path reads is not entirely private to the
vCPU thread: event counters arm cpu->pmu_timer, and arm_pmu_timer_cb()
on the main-loop thread transiently parks the raw cycle count in
c15_ccnt_delta inside its pmu_op_start()/pmu_op_finish() span.
pmu_op_start/finish therefore flag their span via a per-CPU seqlock
(pmu_op_lock), and the fast path validates it before committing, falling
back to the locked path on a race.  It also bails to the slow path for
EL0 reads that PMUSERENR_EL0 denies, so the access trap is still raised
architecturally.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
