2.54.0 (Apple Git-157)
From 63f81f5066001f336c19211746a53dfb91590b25 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: osy <osy@turing.llc>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 08:11:45 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] hvf: use the Hypervisor.framework in-kernel GICv3

On macOS 15+ Hypervisor.framework offers an in-kernel GICv3 (hv_gic_*)
that keeps SGIs, the timer PPIs and every GIC register access inside the
kernel; with the emulated GIC each of those is a VM exit, and a guest
whose job system wakes threads across CPUs pays one per wake.

Add the "hvf-arm-gicv3" device, an ARM_GICV3_COMMON subclass in the
mould of kvm-arm-gicv3 whose MMIO regions are placeholders: SPIs go to
hv_gic_set_spi() and the PPIs QEMU raises itself (the emulated PMU) are
pended into the owning vCPU's redistributor from that vCPU's thread
right before hv_vcpu_run(). hv_gic_set_spi() only marks the interrupt
pending; a vCPU executing guest code does not re-evaluate until its next
exit, so the SPI's affinity route is read from GICD_IROUTER and that
vCPU (every vCPU for a 1-of-N route) is exited with hv_vcpus_exit().

hv_gic_create() must run after the VM exists and before any vCPU is
created, so the virt board creates it right after the GIC version is
finalized, before the CPUs are realized. The hypervisor GIC has no ITS,
so the board disables the ITS and exposes a GICv2m MSI frame instead,
which the MADT now advertises whenever a v2m controller was created.
The accelerator gains "kernel-irqchip=on|off" (default on where
available) and the -machine kernel-irqchip= sugar covers hvf like kvm.

WFI no longer traps with the in-kernel GIC and there are no
VTIMER_ACTIVATED exits; an unexpected one is reported once. Migration
is blocked. Windows 11 and Linux guests boot with it; the PMU overflow
path is not accepted by Windows' sampling profiler (wpr reports the
profile source unsupported), so profiling needs kernel-irqchip=off.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-1
