From 5b298b74f684c14e573bd2298c66754f78abfa16 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: osy <osy@turing.llc>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 01:04:50 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] hvf: save and restore the in-kernel GICv3

Lift the migration blocker on hvf-arm-gicv3 so savevm/loadvm and
migration work with kernel-irqchip=on.

Hypervisor.framework exports the distributor and the redistributors only
as an opaque, versioned blob (hv_gic_state_create/get_data, restored with
hv_gic_set_state); the CPU interfaces are not part of it. The blob travels
in a new "arm_gicv3/kernel_state" subsection of the common GICv3 vmstate,
driven by two optional class hooks (save_kernel_state/load_kernel_state)
that only the HVF model implements. The ICC_* registers are owning-thread
only, so they are read and written on each vCPU with run_on_cpu() into the
existing GICv3CPUState icc_* fields, after the blob on restore.

A failing hv_gic_set_state() (e.g. a state from an incompatible host) fails
the load instead of resuming on a reset GIC, and an emulated GIC refuses a
stream that carries in-kernel state.

Tested on macOS 15.6 with an Ubuntu 25.10 guest, 14 vCPUs: savevm, quit,
-loadvm (twice, the second from a restored instance), a live loadvm, and
migrate to file + -incoming all resume the same boot with timer and IPI
delivery on every vCPU, disk and network working.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
