From e37b2e515d5ce07f09aa34d458baf5c1b0316697 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: osy <osy@turing.llc>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 20:10:52 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] usb-redir: fix up ep0 maxpacket for every device not attached
 at superspeed

A USB 3 device reports bMaxPacketSize0 = 9 (2^9 bytes) in its device
descriptor. That is only valid on a SuperSpeed link, so the descriptor
of a redirected device is rewritten to 64 when the guest sees the device
at a lower speed. A guest that gets the 9 gives up on the device:

  usb 1-1: new high-speed USB device number 2 using xhci_hcd
  usb 1-1: Invalid ep0 maxpacket: 9

The rewrite was limited to devices whose speed is SuperSpeed on a port
without it. Since usb_pick_speed() (b791c3b38c) the speed of an attached
device is the one it runs at on its port, so that is never the case and
the rewrite did not happen at all:

- A SuperSpeed device on a USB 2 controller is attached at high speed.
- A device the usb-host announces with an unknown speed is attached at
  full or high speed. The usb-redir protocol has no speed above
  SuperSpeed, and usbredirhost announces a SuperSpeedPlus (10 Gbit/s)
  device as unknown.

Rewrite the descriptor whenever the device is not attached at
SuperSpeed. A device that really runs below SuperSpeed never has a 9
there, so nothing changes for those.

Based on a change by giovariot.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-2
