From 8ab298bc52158e52bbaf91b7f91b75d46d7384c9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: osy <osy@turing.llc>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 22:03:44 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] usb-redir: attach a device of unknown speed at superspeed if
 it has superspeed endpoints

The usb-redir protocol has no speed above SuperSpeed. usbredirhost
announces a SuperSpeedPlus (10 Gbit/s) device with an unknown speed, and
an unknown speed is attached as full speed. The device itself still runs
at its own speed on the host, so the guest drives a SuperSpeed device
over a full or high speed link. Enumeration can be made to work, but a
USB Attached SCSI drive, which is what most of these devices are, uses
streams on a SuperSpeed link and none below it, and its first command
times out:

  usb 2-3: new high-speed USB device number 3 using ehci-pci
  scsi host0: uas
  scsi 0:0:0:0: tag#16 uas_eh_abort_handler 0 uas-tag 1 inflight: CMD
  scsi 0:0:0:0: Device offlined - not ready after error recovery

Only a SuperSpeed link has bulk endpoints with 1024 byte packets, and
the endpoint info arrives before the connect. Attach a device of unknown
speed that has such an endpoint at SuperSpeed.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-2
