From 9974fed6901e71faa440678f1b2c85dd28d057f3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kaede <kaedetai@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 21:41:06 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-gpu: keep the hostmem BAR decoding after a guest reset

A device reset destroys every resource, and with them every blob mapped
into the hostmem BAR.  The guest driver cannot always revoke the CPU
mappings it handed out before it resets: the Windows display driver
resets the device on its way to D3 at shutdown and reboot, while
user-mode processes (DWM drawing the "Restarting" screen, a game that
is still exiting) can keep writing through their mappings.  Those
writes then land in holes of the BAR.

On hvf such a write is usually a store pair or a SIMD store from
memcpy, which exits without a valid instruction syndrome, so QEMU
aborts on assert(isv) in hvf_handle_exception() and the VM dies on its
way down (UTM reports a QEMULauncher crash when a Windows VM is shut
down after running a D3D12 game).  Under TCG the guest takes an
external abort instead.  A real GPU's BAR keeps decoding.

When a guest-initiated reset finds blobs mapped, put RAM behind the
blob subregions (priority -1, so every blob mapped later shadows it)
and keep it until the next machine reset, after which no guest code
can hold a stale mapping.  A reset with nothing mapped (firmware,
driver start) leaves the BAR untouched, so a normal boot pays nothing.
The RAM is only reserved; host pages are committed only for what the
stale mappings touch.  Guest-initiated and machine resets are told
apart the way virtio_gpu_reset() already does, by whether the reset
runs on a vCPU thread.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
