From 1fe2938b4262c40e82f798f311cb21f35224b05b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kaede <kaedetai@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 13:49:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] target/arm/hvf: retry a data abort on RAM that is mapped
 again

A memory topology update unmaps and then maps again every hvf slot it
touches.  A vCPU that accesses such a range in between takes a stage-2
translation fault, and handles it only after the update is done, once
it gets the BQL; by then the IPA is RAM again.  QEMU still treats the
exit as MMIO, so an access without a valid instruction syndrome (a
store pair or SIMD store from memcpy) aborts on assert(isv), and one
with a syndrome is emulated into RAM.

This is easy to hit with virtio-gpu's hostmem BAR, whose layout changes
whenever a blob is mapped or unmapped while guest processes keep
writing to the blobs that stay: shutting down a Windows guest while an
application is still drawing aborted QEMU this way.

If the IPA is covered by a RAM slot, retry the access instead, as the
x86 hvf code does for EPT violations.  Writes to read-only and
dirty-logged slots still go through emulation.

