From aa44dfaca18b579180c608f60e96dc12630c0b55 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: osy <osy@turing.llc>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 08:49:26 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] ui/spice-display-metal: convert scanouts the surface cannot
 hold verbatim

The Metal scanout path copies the guest's scanout texture into the
shared IOSurface with a blit.  The surface is BGRA8 or RGBA8, but a
scanout can be R10G10B10A2 (a 10-bit fullscreen primary): a blit copies
raw bits, so the 10-bit pixels were shown as BGRA8 (an orange clear came
out blue).

When the scanout is a 10-bit UNORM format (RGB10A2, BGR10A2), draw it
instead: a full-screen triangle whose fragments read the source texel at
the position the blit would copy from, scissored to the damaged rect.
Its values are display-encoded like an 8-bit scanout's, so they are
written as read, with alpha forced opaque (scanout alpha carries
nothing).  The scissor is clamped to the surface and to the scanout
texture past its origin; a 10-bit scanout is never blitted raw (with an
empty rect, or a pipeline that failed to build, the frame is skipped),
and a failed pipeline build is not retried every frame.  A 16-bit-float scanout (normally scRGB: linear, values
above 1.0) would need clamping and sRGB encoding and is left on the blit
path, as is every same-layout format, sRGB twins included.

Measured with exclusive fullscreen at 800x600 cleared orange (1.0, 0.5,
0.0), R10G10B10A2 and R8G8B8A8/B8G8R8A8, blt and flip model: the VM
window shows orange (window capture 239,135,51 after display colour
management); before, the 10-bit cases showed blue (screenshot 50,28,255).

