From e1c17c2009f746a6c1c0a9c6de222f8070a441b9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: osy <osy@turing.llc>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 16:51:06 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] target/ppc: clear the BATs on CPU reset

On 6xx/7xx/74xx CPUs the BAT registers are kept in env->IBAT[] and
env->DBAT[], not in env->spr[]. ppc_cpu_reset_hold() restores env->spr[]
from the SPR defaults and flushes the TLB, but it never touches the BAT
arrays, so they are zero on a cold boot (the CPU state is allocated
zeroed) and keep the guest's last values on every later reset.

OpenBIOS relies on the cold boot state: it turns translation on without
loading the BATs first and expects the next instruction fetch to raise
an ISI, which is how it starts filling its page table. When a guest OS
that has mapped the firmware's address range with a BAT resets the
machine (Mac OS 9 "Restart" on mac99, or system_reset from the
monitor), that fetch goes through the stale mapping instead. OpenBIOS
then executes whatever the guest left there and hangs before it
reinitialises the display, so the restart ends on a black screen:

  CPU Reset (CPU 0)
  Raise exception at fff08b3c => HV_EMU (96) error=21

Clear both arrays in the reset handler so a reset starts from the same
BAT state as a cold boot. This is done for all accelerators: with KVM
the arrays are what kvm_arch_put_registers() pushes to the kernel after
a reset.

Upstream QEMU master has the same omission as of v11.2.0-rc0.

Reported-by: TinBane
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-2
---
 target/ppc/cpu_init.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/target/ppc/cpu_init.c b/target/ppc/cpu_init.c
index 21a76e904a..012957eb39 100644
--- a/target/ppc/cpu_init.c
+++ b/target/ppc/cpu_init.c
@@ -7282,6 +7282,15 @@ static void ppc_cpu_reset_hold(Object *obj, ResetType type)
 #if !defined(CONFIG_USER_ONLY)
     env->nip = env->hreset_vector | env->excp_prefix;
 
+    /*
+     * The BATs live outside env->spr[], so the loop over the SPR defaults
+     * below does not touch them. Clear them here, otherwise the mappings
+     * of the previous boot are still valid when the firmware turns
+     * translation back on after a guest-initiated reset.
+     */
+    memset(env->IBAT, 0, sizeof(env->IBAT));
+    memset(env->DBAT, 0, sizeof(env->DBAT));
+
     if (tcg_enabled()) {
         cpu_breakpoint_remove_all(cs, BP_CPU);
         cpu_watchpoint_remove_all(cs, BP_CPU);
